    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d498">

                <pb facs="0639" n="527" xml:id="pg527"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.1117 American Chinese Trade Commission/4:
                    Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Consul General at
                        Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cunningham</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-08T18:00:00-05:00">January 8, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5. Your No. 9, January 5, noon.<anchor n="29" xml:id="d498-f1"/> According to
                    information furnished the Department, the American Chinese Trade Council, New
                    York, proposes to send to China during the spring of 1935 an unofficial American
                    economic mission for the purpose of studying the possibility of increasing
                    Sino-American trade and the problems therein involved. The Department will
                    inform the Legation and consular officers in China in regard to the personnel
                    and plans of the mission as soon as this information is available.</p>
                <p>Advise Legation and Nanking by mail.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
